{"name":"Green Wrasse","hex":"#13da25","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green wrasse","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fish of species Labrus viridis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green_wrasse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Inscribed wrasse","blocks":[{"text":"The inscribed wrasse is a species of marine ray-finned fish from the family Labridae, the wrasses. It is found in the southwestern Pacific Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Notolabrus_inscriptus_452338032.jpg/330px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_452338032.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inscribed_wrasse","wikidata":"Q2319404"},{"title":"Labrus viridis","blocks":[{"text":"Labrus viridis is a species of wrasse native to the eastern Atlantic Ocean from Portugal to Morocco, as well as through the Mediterranean Sea to the Black Sea. This species is found around rocky reefs amongst the rocks or in eelgrass beds. It can reach 47 cm (19 in) in standard length, though most do not exceed 37.5 cm (15 in). It is one of several species called green wrasse."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Labrus_viridis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labrus_viridis","wikidata":"Q2470153"},{"title":"Wrasse","blocks":[{"text":"The wrasses are a family, Labridae, of marine ray-finned fish, many of which are brightly colored. The family is large and diverse, with over 600 species in 81 genera, which are divided into eight subfamilies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Vieille_coquette_%28Labrus_mixtus%29_%28Ifremer_00563-67488_-_23544%29_%28cropped_2%29.jpg/330px-Vieille_coquette_%28Labrus_mixtus%29_%28Ifremer_00563-67488_-_23544%29_%28cropped_2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrasse","wikidata":"Q208722"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Notolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg/500px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Notolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg/1280px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Notolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg/500px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANotolabrus_inscriptus_365590182.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Notolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg/500px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Notolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg/1280px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Notolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg/500px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"At Norfolk Island","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANotolabrus_inscriptus_166349132.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Notolabrus_inscriptus_Wrasse%2C_Emily_Bay%2C_Kingston_2899%2C_Norfolk_Island_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_244303377_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_Wrasse%2C_Emily_Bay%2C_Kingston_2899%2C_Norfolk_Island_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_244303377_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Notolabrus_inscriptus_Wrasse%2C_Emily_Bay%2C_Kingston_2899%2C_Norfolk_Island_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_244303377_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_Wrasse%2C_Emily_Bay%2C_Kingston_2899%2C_Norfolk_Island_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_244303377_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Notolabrus_inscriptus_Wrasse%2C_Emily_Bay%2C_Kingston_2899%2C_Norfolk_Island_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_244303377_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Notolabrus_inscriptus_Wrasse%2C_Emily_Bay%2C_Kingston_2899%2C_Norfolk_Island_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_244303377_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campai